---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// The eight principles. Held as data so the numbering, the anchor ids and
// the TOC-less page structure stay in one place; the prose is the point,
// so each body is a single paragraph of plain text.
const principles = [
  {
    id: 'later-outcomes-do-not-prove-earlier-intentions',
    heading: 'Later outcomes do not prove earlier intentions.',
    body: `Because Hannibal invaded Italy in 218, it is tempting to read the
      nineteen years of Barcid Iberia as a countdown to it. Because Carthage
      fell in 146, Masinissa's earlier seizures look like steps toward its
      destruction. Both readings run history backward. Intent is treated as
      attested only where a source actually attests it; reconstructed
      intentions are labeled as inference and given only the confidence the
      evidence warrants.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'what-happened-how-it-worked-what-was-intended',
    heading: 'What happened, how it worked, and what was intended are three questions.',
    body: `Evidence for one is not evidence for the next. That Hannibal
      released Italian prisoners is attested. That he did it to detach Rome's
      allies is what Polybius says it was for. What settlement he ultimately
      wanted is much less recoverable, and the site says so rather than
      letting the secure fact carry the speculative one.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'agreement-is-only-as-strong-as-independence',
    heading: 'Agreement between ancient authors is only as strong as the independence of their traditions.',
    body: `Four surviving accounts of Cannae are not four witnesses. Livy used
      Polybius for portions of his Hannibalic narrative; Plutarch and Appian
      drew on traditions that overlap with surviving and lost earlier
      histories. The source comparisons are laid out in parallel columns, and
      that layout can imply an independence the texts do not have.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'a-hostile-source-is-not-a-false-one',
    heading: 'A hostile source is not a false one, and a motive is not a refutation.',
    body: `Polybius moralizes about the rebel leaders of the Mercenary War,
      and his analysis of how that war escalated is still the best thing
      anyone wrote about it, which is why this site's own reading leans on
      him. Showing why an ancient author wanted a story to be true is not the
      same as showing it was not.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'correcting-the-roman-picture',
    heading: 'Correcting the Roman picture is not evidence for its opposite.',
    body: `This is the failure mode a site like this one invites. Carthage's
      dependence on non-citizen troops was real; so was its imperial coercion
      in Africa. Where the traditional account is substantially right, the
      site says so, and says where the generalization built on it goes wrong.
      A revisionist reading gets no presumption in its favor here.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'define-the-term',
    heading: 'Define the term before using it as a conclusion.',
    body: `Empire, state, client, mercenary, province: these words arrive with
      conceptual baggage that can predetermine the answer if they are not
      defined first. The site's position on Barcid Iberia only became arguable
      once "state" meant a durable territorial and fiscal polity rather than a
      sovereign kingdom, and its position on empire only once "empire" meant
      durable unequal power rather than provinces and governors.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'date-and-locate-the-system',
    heading: 'Date and locate the system.',
    body: `Carthaginian power in the sixth century, the fourth, under the
      Barcids and after 201 were different arrangements. So were Carthage's
      relationships with Libya, Utica, Sardinia, Sicily and Iberia; so was the
      army; so was Rome's treatment of Masinissa. A description of how
      Carthage governed that does not say when and where is wrong somewhere.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'where-the-evidence-cannot-decide-stop',
    heading: 'Where the evidence cannot decide, stop.',
    body: `The exact peace Hannibal wanted, the proportion of children at the
      Tophet who were sacrificed, how much contact he had with Antiochus
      before 195: these are not gaps to be closed by picking the most coherent
      story. Open questions are a collection on this site because unresolved
      is an answer.`,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The evidentiary charter"
  description="Eight principles governing how Qart-Hadasht reasons from the surviving evidence for ancient Carthage: on intention, independence of sources, hostile testimony, revisionist correction, definitions, chronology, and where to stop."
>
  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <article class="max-w-3xl min-w-0">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Methodology</p>
      <h1 class="font-serif mb-4">The evidentiary charter</h1>

      <p class="text-lg text-sand-900 leading-snug">
        Most of the literary evidence that survives about Carthage comes
        through Greek and Roman traditions, often from societies that fought
        it, and much of that survives only because later writers found it
        worth preserving. Reconstructing anything under those conditions means
        making judgments, and judgments can go wrong in patterned ways. These
        eight principles are the patterns this site tries to avoid. They are
        also the standard it asks to be held to.
      </p>

      <div class="charter mt-12">
        {principles.map((p, i) => (
          <section class="charter-item">
            <h2 id={p.id}>
              <span class="charter-num" aria-hidden="true">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              {p.heading}
            </h2>
            <p>{p.body}</p>
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="mt-14 pl-5 border-l-4 border-tyrian-700">
        <p class="text-sand-900">
          None of this makes the site pro-Carthage. It is not trying to
          rehabilitate a city; it is trying to reconstruct one under
          evidentiary conditions that are unusually bad and unusually
          one-sided. Where the two aims pull apart, reconstruction wins.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sand-900 mt-4">
          A charter is a claim a site makes about itself, and claims about
          oneself are the hardest ones to check. This one is audited: the
          site's editorial positions are reviewed against the ancient texts in
          passes recorded in its revision history. Every pass so far has found
          something the site stated too strongly, attributed incorrectly, or
          failed to qualify, and therefore something these principles should
          have caught.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <p class="mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-sand-300 text-sm text-sand-700">
        The machinery these principles run on is described on the
        <a href="/methodology" class="text-tyrian-700 underline">methodology page</a>:
        claims and their confidence labels, source distance, stance, and how
        editorial takes and open questions differ. The site's labeled
        positions are collected under
        <a href="/narratives" class="text-tyrian-700 underline">narratives and synthesis</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The principles are full sentences rather than short section labels, so
     they take a smaller, lighter heading than the standard prose h2 (which
     carries a 2px ink rule at clamp 1.55–2.15rem and would read as eight
     chapter openings). Hairline rule, Bricolage 700, tyrian number in the
     site's graphic system. */
  .charter-item + .charter-item { margin-top: 2.2rem; }
  .charter-item h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 1.32rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    padding-top: 1.1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }
  .charter-item:first-child h2 { padding-top: 0; border-top: none; }
  .charter-num {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--color-tyrian);
    margin-right: 0.5rem;
  }
  .charter-item p {
    max-width: 68ch;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
</style>
